data: {"id":"chatcmpl-ecaeeae6-ffe7-4ed6-8022-f25e7898ee05","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544954,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The logic for savepoint removal during rollbacks is being carefully reviewed. The crucial invariant is that savepoint positions, ordered by increasing set offsets, maintain non-decreasing positions within the write list; this is due to how writes, savepoint additions, and rollbacks interact. Adding writes, setting a savepoint, and performing rollbacks were analyzed and confirmed to preserve this property, as demonstrated through induction on time. This invariant ensures that savepoints are correctly removed according to their set offsets. Confirmed that R23c (CHECKPOINT) logic requires no changes.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-ecaeeae6-ffe7-4ed6-8022-f25e7898ee05","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544954,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The read set implementation is straightforward, directly adding the key to the transaction's read set as required.  Checked the `Result`'s `state` to handle immutability using standard `Map` implementations.  The choice is between `Collections.unmodifiableMap(state)` and Java 21's immutable collections, such as `Map.of()` and `Map.copyOf()`.  Crucially, Java's `Map.copyOf()` uses randomized iteration order which violates the explicit deterministic behavior requirement.  Confirmed that `Collections.unmodifiableMap(new LinkedHashMap<>(state))` preserves deterministic iteration order, avoiding any non-deterministic behavior arising from hash iteration order, as per the rules.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

