data: {"id":"chatcmpl-ecaeeae6-ffe7-4ed6-8022-f25e7898ee05","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544954,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Rethinking transaction ID and key validation order. Initial assumption that `BAD_TXN_ID` would be raised first is incorrect; the strict order of checks (R13, R14, R15) means `MALFORMED_PAYLOAD` precedes `BAD_TXN_ID` and `BAD_KEY` exceptions. Validated with an example (k=200, N=10) that `MALFORMED_PAYLOAD` (R13) is indeed thrown before any other specific errors.\n\nReviewed payload validation for DELETE (Type 3): payload size `N` checks for `N >= 5`, then extracts key length `k` and validates `N == 5 + k`. For SAVEPOINT/ROLLBACK (Types 6, 7), the size constraint is `N == 5`. For CHECKPOINT (Type 8), `N >= 1` is enforced, followed by the number of checkpoints `c` and validation of `N == 1 + 4 * c`.\n\nConfirmed READ record (Type 9) validation logic: requires `N >= 6`, extracts key length `k`, validates `N < 6 + k`, and finally extracts the flag `f` and its index (`5+k`) within bounds. This ensures all indexing operations are safe based on the payload size.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

